findings.body:
The PR changes `scheduling.init({apiUrl: urlUtils.urlFor('api', {type: 'admin'}, true)})` to `scheduling.init()`. The scheduler needs that `apiUrl`. `post-scheduling/index.js` forwards `options.apiUrl` to `new PostScheduler(...)`, and `PostScheduler`'s constructor throws `IncorrectUsageError('post-scheduling: no apiUrl was provided')` when it is missing. Here the call is inside the `Promise.all` in `initServices()`, so the rejection propagates and boot fails before the server listens.

::code{file="ghost/core/core/boot.js" lines="362-372"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The `urlUtils` require at line 343 is still present and would be the value needed to restore the argument.

Restore the original argument:

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/boot.js" lines="368-368"}
scheduling.init({
    apiUrl: urlUtils.urlFor('api', {type: 'admin'}, true)
}),

The original boot list had `xmlrpc.listen()` followed by `slack.listen()`. The PR removes both, but the xmlrpc deletion is the only one the PR describes. `slack.listen()` is what registers `slackListener` on `post.published` and `slackTestPing` on `slack.test` (see `server/services/slack.js:192-201`). With it gone, Slack post-published notifications silently stop working and nothing logs an error. The `slack` require at line 319 is left unused, which will likely trip the `no-unused-vars` lint rule.

::code{file="ghost/core/core/boot.js" lines="317-321"}

The existing `slack.test.js` calls `slack.listen()` directly, so it still passes and would not catch this regression.

`emailAddressService.init()` was a standalone `await` so that it finished before the parallel group. The PR moves it into the `Promise.all` and leaves the comment at line 349 saying the newsletter and email services depend on it. This works today only because `EmailAddressServiceWrapper.init()` is synchronous and array elements are evaluated left to right, so `emailAddressService.service` is set before `emailService.init()` reads it in `EmailServiceWrapper.init()`. The guarantee now depends on the order of entries in an array literal, and nothing enforces it. The comment also refers to a sequence that no longer exists.

::code{file="ghost/core/core/boot.js" lines="349-353"}

If someone makes `init()` async later, or reorders the array, the email service will silently receive `undefined` for the address service.
